---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Robots.txt basics — AISEOOnline"
  description="Understand how robots.txt controls crawling, what it doesn't do, and common mistakes to avoid."
  type="article"
>
  <article class="section-space bg-white">
    <div class="site-container max-w-3xl">
      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm text-slate-600">
        <a class="inline-link" href="/guides/">Guides</a><span class="mx-2" aria-hidden="true"
          >/</span
        ><span aria-current="page">Robots.txt basics</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="eyebrow mt-12">Technical SEO</p>
      <h1 class="section-title mt-4">Robots.txt: what it can and can't do</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">
        The robots.txt file helps crawlers understand which URL paths they may request. It isn't a
        guarantee that a page will disappear from search results.
      </p>
      <div class="prose-article mt-12">
        <h2>What is robots.txt?</h2>
        <p>
          It's a plain-text file typically served at the root of a website, such as <code
            >https://example.com/robots.txt</code
          >. A crawler that supports the Robots Exclusion Protocol reads it to determine which paths
          it should avoid crawling.
        </p>
        <h2>A simple example</h2>
        <pre><code>User-agent: *
Disallow: /private-preview/
Allow: /</code></pre>
        <p>
          This asks cooperating crawlers to avoid URLs under <code>/private-preview/</code>. It does
          not authenticate visitors or protect the content from direct access.
        </p>
        <h2>Crawling is not indexing</h2>
        <p>
          A disallowed URL can still appear in search results when other pages link to it. If the
          goal is to prevent indexing, use an appropriate indexing directive on a page the crawler
          is allowed to access, or restrict access to genuinely private information.
        </p>
        <h2>Common checks</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Confirm the file is reachable at the expected host and path.</li><li>
            Check rule groups and path patterns for unexpected conflicts.
          </li><li>Do not block pages that need to be fetched to see an indexing directive.</li><li>
            Use authentication for private resources; robots.txt is not access control.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Different crawlers may interpret unsupported rules differently. Test with the
          documentation and tools for the crawler that matters to you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a
        class="mt-10 inline-block font-semibold text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4"
        href="/guides/">← Back to guides</a
      >
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
